  {
    "sha": "776cc265d8dfa692e9169664e3e1acef4215f0df",
    "node_id": "C_kwDOVoU8JdoAKDc3NmNjMjY1ZDhkZmE2OTJlOTE2OTY2NGUzZTFhY2VmNDIxNWYwZGY",
    "commit": {
      "author": {
        "name": "Mike Kinney",
        "email": "mike.kinney@gmail.com",
        "date": "2026-11-21T01:46:38Z"
      },
      "committer": {
        "name": "Mike Kinney",
        "email": "mike.kinney@gmail.com",
        "date": "2026-11-21T01:46:38Z"
      },
      "message": "fix(motion): turning offset claims a segment only where its tangent point lies on it\n\nThe nose offset of the turned profile held each segment's offset\nline over the segment's own station window. A steep, short fillet\nchord then claimed its infinite line far above the bar (X354 on a\n30 mm bar), and a sloped face missed the stations before its inside\ncorner, where the ride stood nearer than the nose radius.\n\nThe line claim now holds over the window where the ball's tangent\npoint is on the segment: the own window shifted by d*b/sqrt(1+b^2).\nThe end point circles claim the rest. Both the OD ride and the bore\nride use the same rule.\n\nThe catalog programs turning/cone-thread and turning/contour-finishes\nare regenerated: the zero-length blocks at the cone corner go, the\nrides roll over the inside corner on the nose circle, and the\ncontour-finishes clearance radius drops from X22.884 to X21.6.\n\nFixes #2881\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
